<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexander II of Imereti</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1510">1510</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexander II was king of Imereti in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> from 1484 to 1510, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrat-vi-of-georgia">Bagrat II of Imereti</a>. He held the kingdom against rivals and was recognised as overlord by the princes of Mingrelia, Guria and Abkhazia, though his attempts to take Kartli failed. In the last year of his reign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> raided Imereti for the first time and sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-imereti.jpg" alt="Alexander II of Imereti" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This image has been extracted from another file · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_II_of_Imereti_(crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alexander was the third son of Bagrat and his wife Helen, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagrationi-dynasty">Bagrationi dynasty</a>. His father, king of Imereti since 1463, moved to Kartli in 1466 and declared himself King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> as Bagrat VI, and in 1468 he agreed with his rival Prince Constantine that on his death Constantine would have Kartli and Alexander Imereti. When Bagrat died in 1478, Alexander tried to take both. His coronation at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a> failed when Vameq II of Mingrelia, Kakhaber II of Guria and the nobles of Abkhazia and Svaneti stayed away, and Constantine II took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, then Gori and Kutaisi by 1479. Alexander withdrew to the mountains of Racha and Lechkhumi and fought on from there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After the death of Vameq II, Alexander retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a> in 1484 and was crowned King of Likht-Imereti, the land on this side of the Likhi Range. He defeated Constantine at Chikhori in 1485, lost his domains to a renewed invasion in 1487, and regained them when attacks by Sultan Yaqub Aq Qoyunlu drew Constantine east; Kutaisi fell to his mountain allies in 1489. In 1490 the Georgian nobility dissolved the unified kingdom, and a treaty with Constantine confirmed Imereti's independence. To win peace, Alexander freed Liparit of Mingrelia and George of Guria from taxes and royal interference, while they gave soldiers to his army and owed him hereditary court offices.</p><p>After Constantine died in 1505, Alexander crossed the Likhi Range in 1509, took Gori and annexed north-western Kartli. In his absence an Ottoman army under Selim, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> and son of the sultan, invaded on 23 November 1509, burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a> and the Gelati Monastery and carried off captives as slaves. Alexander abandoned Gori, and David X of Kartli recovered the lost lands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Queen Tamar died in March 1510, and Alexander, who had been planning a campaign into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, fell ill and died on 1 April 1510. He was buried beside her at Gelati and succeeded by his young son Bagrat III. He maintained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicate-of-abkhazia">Catholicate of Abkhazia</a>, naming Stephan catholicos in 1490, and under his rule Spanish Jews expelled in 1492 settled in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. An inscription calls him King of Kings of Likht-Imer and Likht-Amier, a title his successors kept until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> annexed Imereti in 1810.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexander II of Imereti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.27,42.71&amp;year=1500" aria-label="Kutaisi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5586 660 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Alexander II of Imereti"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6036 930h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6036 930h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=42.27,42.71&amp;year=1500">What was here in 1500? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
